```

Canonical new Parquet schema uses `parliament_member_id: UInt32` for member ownership everywhere it is stored; remove UUID-valued `member_id` columns. Raw partition filenames are canonical decimal `<parliament_member_id>.parquet`, including zero-row partitions. Declaration and parent fields keep their source IDs. Occurrence keys retain the exact existing delimiter and layout `member/declaration/register/funding/ordinal`, with only `member` replaced by the canonical source member decimal ID; observation suffixes retain their existing role layout. Resolved identities derived from observations thereby become portable. Run UUIDs remain run IDs and never appear as member/declaration components. New schema versions distinguish these layouts, and all writers emit only the new layout.

The raw reader accepts a legacy UUID filename/column only for decoding existing captures. It extracts `parliament_member_id` from nonempty rows, checks agreement across rows, and verifies old filename/old column coherence without consulting the database. The six empty old UUID partitions have no recoverable member source ID: decode them only as empty evidence and omit them from new declaration rows because they contain no declarations. Never invent a member mapping or query current UUIDs. This restriction is explicit, and preserves all nonempty evidence. New empty partitions are always recoverable by source filename. The legacy reader must not re-emit an old UUID into any new output.

Reject old cleaned/resolved runs with an actionable “reclean raw captures and resolve again” schema error. There is no alternate legacy internal API, no old/new key union, and no mixed-format run. Load replay uses the same normalized raw reader to validate regenerated artifacts. Existing raw manifest checks/digests apply to the original retained bytes; regenerated clean/resolved manifests bind their own new schemas and digests.

In `DeclarationLoadRepository::load_declarations`, after starting the existing transaction, collect distinct `MemberId`s and use `sqlx::query!` to select current `id, parliament_member_id` by source IDs, with row locking consistent with publication. Build one adapter-private map from typed source ID to current UUID. Missing bindings fail the entire transaction before any publication; resolve each declaration’s member from this map during insertion. Database UUIDs remain local variables in this adapter. Preserve exact-retry fingerprints, conflict checks, stale timestamp rules, rollback, and existing declaration upsert semantics. No migration is needed.

Migrate together: Parliament member API decoding, member parquet decoding, member search/database row decoding, constructors and tests; fetch repository/API/storage signatures; raw reader/writer; cleaner records and key construction; resolution inputs, parent comparison, observation matching and worker contract; load replay/checks/types and SQL adapter; Python fixtures and resolver tests; Rust integration tests; cleaning/resolution documentation. Delete every old `MemberAsId`, captured UUID lookup, UUID member parser, and duplicate member allowlist/check.

## Synthesis decision

Candidate only; the orchestrator selects the base and grafts alternatives.

## Tradeoffs accepted

- We accept invalidating old derived artifacts in exchange for one canonical identity model and no UUID-bearing compatibility path downstream.
- We accept a narrowly scoped legacy raw decoder in exchange for recovering retained nonempty captures without API acquisition.
- We accept changing ParliamentMember construction callers in exchange for making unchecked MemberId creation impossible.

## Alternatives considered

A binding-envelope pipeline could carry both source ID and optional local database UUID, then strip UUIDs in each writer. It hides lookup convenience but exposes identity selection to every transformation and writer; a nearest-example copy can leak UUIDs again. It loses on invariants and interface depth.

An offline artifact rewrite tool could migrate all old raw/clean/resolved keys before loading. Its single command hides a complex graph rewrite, but duplicates raw schema and cross-reference knowledge outside the owning adapters and requires users to coordinate rewrite and load. Reader normalization plus regenerated derived output keeps one authoritative transformation path.

## Implementation reconciliation

No implementation yet. Any deviation affecting artifact keys, legacy acceptance, member construction, or transaction binding must update this contract before subsequent units consume it.

## Open questions and risks

Do existing resolver worker prompts or fixtures assume UUID-shaped observation IDs? Trace and change them alongside Rust so worker results still reference exactly the supplied source keys.

## Next implementation step

Make canonical MemberId construction checked, migrate member acquisition constructors, and replace MemberAsId in fetch/storage signatures before producing the source-only raw schema.

## Verification contract

Use one regenerated dataset in two disposable databases with identical source member IDs and distinct database UUIDs. Load both and assert source declaration/member joins and declaration/funder/funding counts match; source artifacts, occurrence IDs, observation IDs, and resolution identities remain byte-identical. Repeat load for AlreadyLoaded; inject a missing source member and a transactional write failure to prove no partial publication; retain stale timestamp checks. Exercise the supplied real CLI against the rebuilt disposable database. Test zero/overflow IDs at API/parquet/database boundaries, wrong member partition, conflicting legacy source owners, empty new capture, and legacy nonempty replay without API calls. Inspect every newly written Parquet schema/value and all derived key components for captured database UUID absence. Red-flag screen: one owner per source rule/key formatter, no importable legacy internals, no UUID-bearing domain type, no dual derived schema, and one database publication operation.

